EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-24e2o1_document-udp-limits-metrics-and-operations/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-1nsw9p_udp-forwarding-and-associations/TASK-260715-24e2o1_document-udp-limits-metrics-and-operations/README.md:10:1. Diagrams and tables trace one datagram and response through every component and state who owns frame, association, buffer, socket, timer, and cleanup at each boundary. 2. Exact configured and negotiated limits, hard ceilings, admission order, priority classes, saturation drops, error codes, idle expiry, and ID reuse conditions match implemented tests and diagnostics. 3. DNS documentation covers approved resolver, UDP relay priority, TC and safe TCP fallback, cache interaction, failure behavior, and zero physical resolver path. 4. Reproduction sections name local, conformance, fuzz, pressure, soak, and Apple-silicon Mac commands, marking iPhone commands as deferred under ADR-024, with expected counters, captures, memory fields, and redaction steps. 5. Troubleshooting covers address, oversize, exhaustion, queue pressure, resolution, socket, relay process, protocol, and cleanup failures and links capability, M3, and release handoffs by board ID.
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3kga9i_document-capability-modes-limitations-and-diagnostics/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3kga9i_document-capability-modes-limitations-and-diagnostics/README.md:10:1. Mode and transition diagrams match the implemented state contract and show exact readiness, cleanup, retry, full restoration, and failed boundaries. 2. A traffic table states what happens to new and existing TCP, UDP, DNS, and relevant QUIC-family traffic in every state and accurately discloses Apple system exceptions without a cryptographically absolute kill-switch claim. 3. The finite reason catalog maps each cause to mode, user-safe summary, operator diagnostics, retry eligibility, and remediation without destinations, queries, payloads, credentials, or remote-controlled strings. 4. Reproduction sections link state, fault, leak, harness, and Apple-silicon Mac evidence, naming iPhone evidence as deferred under ADR-024, and list expected snapshots, captures, memory, counters, and cleanup results. 5. Concrete handoffs identify M3 lane, path, NAT64, sleep, captive, fail-closed, and QUIC work plus M4 status UI and privacy copy, and the document does not claim those later behaviors exist.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/README.md:10:Gate A0 is deferred off this epic's critical path under ADR-013 and stays required before App Store distribution and iOS submission, where it must have documented evidence; Gate P0 installs a packet-tunnel provider on the physical Apple-silicon Mac; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass; packet bridge/HEV and one SSH engine pass the M0 matrix; open decisions are recorded with reproducible results.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md-3-## Description
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:4:Prove that the Relux Works organization can provision the required host and packet-tunnel extension identities and can install and launch a minimally functional custom VPN provider on the physical Apple-silicon Mac. iOS is deferred under ADR-024; its identifiers stay defined so the iPhone row re-arms unchanged. The result is a reproducible signing and entitlement baseline for later M0 integration, not a production VPN.
