EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-3r0993_select-project-generator-and-deployment-target-policy/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/TASK-260715-3r0993_select-project-generator-and-deployment-target-policy/README.md:10:1. A dated TASK-ID-scoped research artifact compares the supported generator choices against deterministic output, extension targets, signing configuration, dependency integration, CI maintenance, and Relux Works conventions. 2. The chosen tool and version or version range, pinning mechanism, bootstrap command, and upgrade owner are explicit. 3. Exact minimum iOS and macOS versions are selected from current API, dependency, device, Xcode, and CI evidence rather than preference. 4. The policy names the current physical iPhone and Apple-silicon Mac baseline, records the iPhone baseline as deferred with iOS under ADR-024 while keeping it defined so it re-arms unchanged, plus the oldest versions that must build and test. 5. Any unsupported assumption becomes a blocking research item instead of a speculative configuration.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/README.md:10:1. A task-scoped protocol defines reproducible workloads, network impairments, warmup, duration, samples, statistical comparison, pass or fail rules, metadata, raw artifact references, redaction, and regression thresholds. 2. Low-overhead bounded instrumentation reconciles packet, transport, DNS, relay, lane, memory, CPU, energy, error, and cleanup counters without payloads, destinations, query names, or high-cardinality identifiers. 3. Untuned Apple-silicon Mac baselines, with iPhone rows deferred-unavailable under ADR-024, cover required MTUs, lane counts, window profiles, traffic shapes, concurrency stages, loss and latency, lifecycle, and the provisional HEV configuration. 4. Each accepted parameter change has before-and-after evidence and preserves byte correctness, latency bounds, route safety, zero ordinary DNS fallback, bounded queues, and the extension memory ceiling. 5. The 25 to 30 MiB steady-state target is reported as measured engineering evidence rather than an Apple guarantee, with peak, reconnect overlap, available-memory samples, and any device variance. 6. A HEV fork is approved only if Instruments identifies bridge copies or syscall rate as a material bottleneck and a minimal upstream-trackable change demonstrates meaningful improvement with regression coverage. 7. The final named-device matrix passes lane congestion, rekey, memory pressure, path changes, NAT64, sleep and wake, captive, QUIC, and both route modes or records explicit red rows and blocking follow-up work, and publishes accepted baselines and thresholds.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-2kchi0_record-performance-measurement-protocol/README.md-6-## Scope
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-2kchi0_record-performance-measurement-protocol/README.md:7:In scope: the named Apple-silicon Mac baseline, with the named iPhone baseline deferred with iOS under ADR-024; OS, toolchain, source and dependency revisions; profile and server config; MTU, HEV, lane, window, rekey, QUIC, route, reconnect, and memory parameters; web-like, bulk, idle, DNS, UDP burst, mixed, and concurrency workloads; loss and latency; lifecycle and failure rows; warmup, steady state, repetitions, outliers, confidence or practical-effect rule; throughput, TTFB, DNS latency, RTT, CPU, energy, memory, packet and syscall rate, queues, drops, cleanup; raw evidence manifest and redaction. Out of scope: running benchmarks, choosing results in advance, public-user traffic, unmeasured tuning, marketing claims, or hiding red rows.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-2kchi0_record-performance-measurement-protocol/README.md-8-
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-21c23x_quic-and-route-policies/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-21c23x_quic-and-route-policies/README.md:10:1. Allow QUIC forwards eligible UDP/443 only through a current validated relay generation, Block UDP/443 returns the documented fast local failure, and neither mode silently times out or affects unrelated UDP or tunnel DNS. 2. Auto uses versioned lane-health inputs, deterministic thresholds and hysteresis, rejects new UDP/443 quickly when QUIC-over-SSH is predicted to underperform, and records only aggregate reasons. 3. Full, degraded, reasserting, failed, stopping, and stale-generation states each have one explicit UDP/443 outcome with no physical socket fallback. 4. Compatible mode installs dual-stack defaults and only approved exact exclusions, while fail-closed mode enables includeAllNetworks only where supported and exposes the documented system exceptions without absolute claims. 5. Startup, live mode change, endpoint replacement, reconnect, settings failure, and stop apply or roll back route and DNS settings without a recursive route, broad exclusion, false-connected snapshot, or ordinary DNS leak. 6. Unit, fault, and golden tests cover policy decisions, fast-failure latency, unrelated traffic, platform branches, exact routes, rollback, and redaction. 7. Physical Apple-silicon Mac captures, with iPhone rows deferred-unavailable under ADR-024, across compatible and fail-closed modes and full, degraded, and reasserting capabilities match the disclosed traffic matrix and record OS behavior changes.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2indo6_ssh-lane-pool-and-scheduler/README.md-9-## Acceptance Criteria
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2indo6_ssh-lane-pool-and-scheduler/README.md:10:1. One mandatory control lane starts first and optional general or bulk lanes open only after identity, server-limit, and memory admission checks. 2. Every lane proves the same approved host identity and credential generation, and any mismatch closes that lane before channels open. 3. New flows are assigned from documented congestion and policy inputs, remain pinned until close, and Lane D never retroactively adopts an existing flow. 4. Control, relay, and latency-sensitive DNS remain serviceable under general or bulk congestion within recorded latency bounds. 5. Rekey and failed-lane states stop new assignment on the affected lane, preserve valid existing channel state where possible, and route control-lane loss through an explicit degraded or recovery transition without DNS or UDP leakage. 6. Queues, lanes, and channels stay inside configured ceilings and soft memory pressure suppresses optional capacity. 7. Deterministic tests and physical Apple-silicon Mac trials, with iPhone rows deferred-unavailable under ADR-024, at one, two, and four lanes record loss, latency, throughput, queues, windows, channels, memory, CPU, errors, and cleanup and demonstrate measured head-of-line improvement without resource growth.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md-3-## Description
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:4:Harden and validate the M3 runtime across IPv6-only NAT64, address-family changes, sleep and wake, captive-network transitions, containing-app termination, provider stops, and repeated lifecycle loops on the named physical Apple-silicon Mac configuration; the physical-iPhone configurations are deferred with iOS under ADR-024 as named gaps that are never inferred from Mac results.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md-5-
